%% SECTION Stakeholders and Communications
@@
When should stakeholder identification begin?
A) As early as possible, usually once the charter is approved, and repeatedly afterwards
B) After the project management plan is approved, when all of the stakeholders' roles are clear
C) During planning, once the requirements have been collected and documented
D) Only when a stakeholder raises a concern or requests a change to the project
= A
? Identify Stakeholders is an initiating process, and identification is repeated throughout as the project and environment change.

@@
A stakeholder has low power but high interest — for example, a group of end users. What is the usual strategy?
A) Keep informed
B) Manage closely
C) Keep satisfied
D) Ignore
= A
? Low power, high interest stakeholders are kept informed; they can also become important allies.

@@
Which communication method is most appropriate for an announcement that must reach all 2,000 employees and confirm delivery?
A) Push communication, such as an email sent to all employees
B) Pull communication, such as a notice on the intranet home page
C) Interactive communication, through one meeting per department
D) Informal communication, through managers' hallway conversations
= A
? Push communication is sent to specific recipients and ensures distribution, although not necessarily understanding.

@@
What does the communication model term "noise" refer to?
A) Anything that interferes with sending or understanding a message
B) The loudness of the speaker's voice during a presentation or a meeting
C) The feedback the receiver returns to confirm understanding
D) The total number of communication channels in the project
= A
? Noise includes distance, technology problems, language, culture, distractions and other barriers.

@@ | fill
A project team has 15 members including the project manager. How many communication channels exist?
= 105
? Channels = n(n − 1) / 2 = 15 × 14 / 2 = 105.

@@
Which document records each stakeholder's interests, influence, expectations and classification?
A) Stakeholder register
B) Stakeholder engagement plan
C) Issue log
D) Communications management plan
= A
? The register contains identification, assessment and classification information. The engagement plan describes strategies to involve stakeholders.

@@
A powerful department head is resistant to the project. The project manager's analysis shows she fears losing headcount. What is the best approach?
A) Meet her to understand her concerns and explore how the project can address them
B) Limit her involvement until implementation, so that her resistance does not spread to others
C) Ask the sponsor to instruct her to support the project and stop objecting
D) Raise her resistance in the steering meeting so others can pressure her
= A
? Understanding interests and addressing concerns is the most effective way to move a resistant stakeholder toward support.

@@
Which stakeholder analysis model considers power, urgency and legitimacy?
A) Salience model
B) Power/interest grid
C) Stakeholder cube
D) Engagement assessment matrix
= A
? The salience model classifies stakeholders on these three attributes.

@@
A stakeholder engagement assessment matrix shows a stakeholder as "Unaware" (current) and "Supportive" (desired). What should be the first engagement objective?
A) Make the stakeholder aware of the project and its impact on them
B) Invite the stakeholder to lead the project's steering committee from now on
C) Escalate to the sponsor to obtain the stakeholder's support
D) Keep the stakeholder unaware to avoid creating resistance
= A
? Moving a stakeholder from unaware requires communication to build awareness before support can develop.

@@
The sponsor complains that the status reports contain too much detail and asks for a one-page summary. What should the project manager do?
A) Agree on a summary dashboard and update the communications management plan accordingly
B) Continue sending the detailed reports, since they contain everything the sponsor might need
C) Stop sending reports to the sponsor and rely on the monthly steering committee instead
D) Send the detailed reports more often so the sponsor gets used to the level of detail
= A
? Communication must meet stakeholder needs. Feedback triggers an update to the communications approach and plan.

@@ | multi
Which two practices support communication in a distributed agile team? (Choose two.)
A) Shared digital task boards visible to all members
B) Agreed overlap hours for synchronous collaboration
C) Replacing all conversations with long written reports
D) Holding the daily scrum only for co-located members
E) Removing video from all calls
= A, B
? Digital information radiators and overlapping working hours help distributed teams collaborate. Excluding remote members or relying only on reports weakens communication.

@@
Which communication dimension is used when a project manager discusses a sensitive personnel issue with a team member?
A) Informal, private, interactive conversation
B) Formal written report sent to all of the stakeholders
C) Pull communication on a wiki
D) Push email to the entire team
= A
? Sensitive issues are best handled privately and interactively, allowing dialogue and preserving dignity.

@@
A stakeholder suddenly opposes a decision that was agreed in a meeting last month. What could best have prevented misunderstanding?
A) Documenting decisions and actions and circulating minutes for confirmation
B) Holding fewer meetings so that decisions are not revisited so often
C) Making the decision without the stakeholders, so that later disagreements are avoided
D) Communicating decisions only verbally to keep the process flexible
= A
? Documented and confirmed decisions reduce misunderstandings and provide a reference when disputes arise.

@@
Which process verifies that communications are having the intended effect and meeting stakeholders' information needs?
A) Monitor Communications
B) Plan Communications Management
C) Manage Communications
D) Identify Stakeholders
= A
? Monitor Communications checks the effectiveness of communications and triggers adjustments.

@@
The project manager learns that a key user group in another country prefers indirect communication and avoids open disagreement in meetings. What should the project manager do?
A) Adapt how input is gathered, for example individual or anonymous feedback
B) Insist that they voice disagreement openly in the large requirements meetings
C) Interpret their silence in meetings as agreement with the proposed requirements
D) Exclude them from requirements sessions and send them the final documents
= A
? Cultural awareness means adapting methods so stakeholders can contribute in ways comfortable for them.

@@
Which of the following is an example of pull communication?
A) A knowledge repository that stakeholders consult when they need it
B) A weekly status email sent to everyone on the project's distribution list
C) A video conference with the steering committee members
D) A telephone call to update the sponsor on a key decision
= A
? Pull communication lets recipients access information at their discretion, suitable for large volumes or audiences.

@@
Which stakeholder direction of influence describes relationships with peer project managers competing for the same resources?
A) Sideward
B) Upward
C) Downward
D) Outward
= A
? Sideward influence concerns peers, such as other project managers or middle managers.

@@
A regulator is an important stakeholder whose approval is required. Which engagement approach is most appropriate?
A) Engage early and regularly to understand requirements and avoid surprises
B) Contact the regulator once the product is complete and ready for approval
C) Ask the team to interpret the regulations themselves to save time and cost
D) Treat the regulator as a low priority, since regulators are external parties
= A
? Early engagement with high-power stakeholders such as regulators prevents costly late rework and delays.

@@
What is the project manager's main goal when managing stakeholder expectations?
A) Keep expectations aligned with what can realistically be delivered
B) Agree to stakeholders' requests so that satisfaction remains high
C) Minimise contact with stakeholders so that fewer new requests arise
D) Leave expectation management to the sponsor, who owns the business case
= A
? Expectation management is continuous: surface expectations, negotiate trade-offs and communicate transparently.

@@
Which technique helps a speaker confirm that a listener understood a complex message?
A) Asking the listener to paraphrase the key points
B) Speaking faster
C) Sending the same message twice by email, a day apart
D) Avoiding questions
= A
? Paraphrasing and feedback are part of active listening and confirm shared understanding.
